when I was a graduate student I had no  real conception of the time scale  involved in math research for me in  everything I've done it's been very slow  you get into a feel takes a long time to  learn what's the what's going on long  time to figure out what you should be  working on a long time to solve it and  then sometimes at that point you you  understand things a bit better you  realize well that wasn't the right thing  to have been working on so for me that  that time scale has been five to ten  years in the things I've done and I just  had no conception of that both at length  of time when I was a graduate student my  working sounds very collaborative  I've had many co-authors and I've work  with them for a long time for me there  are two really important aspects of that  one is that in a group usually someone  has an idea so it has much more of a  sense of continuous progress and when  you're by yourself and very often you're  you have longer periods of being stuck  by yourself the second which I think is  more important if then is the social  aspect when you're doing math by  yourself it can be very isolating and  when you work in a group it turns that  individual activity into something  that's much more social and pleasant and  human so for me that aspect has been  really important to my career
 

